I’ll first inspect the metadata, diffraction range, and the provided P0/GSAS-II workflow, then build a phase inventory from chemistry and peak evidence before refinement. I’ll keep the final exports tied to a reopened, replayed `final.gpx` so the reported fractions and profile are reproducible.

The chemical context points to the Sr–W–O system after a 900 °C, 1 h firing, so SrWO₄ is the leading product candidate, with SrCO₃/WO₃ or nonstoichiometric Sr–W oxides as plausible residuals. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the frozen COD index for exact and analogue structures; candidate rejection will be based on unmatched coherent peak families, not scale alone.

Peak-family matching already supports tetragonal scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃; the 23.5/24.0/24.7° triplet is especially diagnostic for WO₃. A separate strong family near 29.76° is not explained by either, so I’m treating SrO and Sr-rich tungstate intermediates as live alternatives while I establish a common GSAS-II background/position/profile model.

The unmodeled intensity is not consistent with SrO or SrCO₃, and its strongest families correlate with a monoclinic Sr–W–O framework. However, the matching COD SrWO₄ record is an ~11 GPa high-pressure phase, so it fails the source-condition screen for this ambient box-furnace sample. Stoichiometric balance (1:1 Sr:W) plus the refined WO₃ minority instead points to an Sr-rich intermediate—most plausibly Sr₂WO₅—so I’m testing charge-matched Sr₂WO₅ analogue prototypes rather than accepting the high-pressure decoy.

The three-phase inventory is now coherent: scheelite SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and an analogue-derived monoclinic Sr₂WO₅ model all refine to positive scales, with provisional fractions near 78/10/12 wt%. I’m now replacing the earlier unconstrained width fit—which drove the instrumental U term negative—with bounded nonnegative U/W/X plus phase-by-phase size/strain tests; the fractions are not final until that baseline is stable.

The first bounded pass confirmed why the earlier profile was misleading: W and X both tried to exceed the laboratory range, indicating unresolved phase-specific broadening rather than a valid instrumental solution. I’ve switched to rollback trials—each cell, size, strain, or asymmetry term is retained only if it improves the reopened full-range project—so rejected nuisance terms cannot contaminate later fractions.

The Sr₂WO₅ analogue is genuinely improving the intensity fit when its heavy-atom coordinates are allowed to adapt, but unconstrained refinement creates W–O contacts as short as 1.27 Å, so I am rejecting those numerical minima. I’ve repaired the cross-chemistry prototype to four symmetry-related 1.78 Å W–O bonds and verified the resulting P2₁/c model passes the contact/density screen before re-fitting it.

I’ve narrowed the inventory to scheelite SrWO₄ plus monoclinic WO₃ and a weak Sr-rich tungstate candidate. I’m now challenging that third phase against physical analogues and source-incompatible polymorphs, then I’ll lock a bounded, replayable refinement and verify the exported project/profile agree exactly.

The alternate checks are decisive in two directions: a Sr₃WO₆ analogue does not explain the residuals, while a monoclinic SrWO₄ record fits better numerically but is documented at about 11 GPa, incompatible with this ambient 900 °C synthesis. I’m treating that as a useful decoy rather than silently accepting it, and I’m using the generator/lab constraints plus peak-family evidence to decide whether the weak phase can be reported structurally or must be marked provisional.

The chemistry now points to a stronger hypothesis: the fitted weak-phase/WO₃ mass ratio is close to the exact balance expected for `Sr₃WO₆ + 2 WO₃ → 3 SrWO₄`, whereas it is inconsistent with the earlier Sr₂WO₅ assignment. The first Sr₃WO₆ analogue I tried had the wrong topology, so I’m screening several same-stoichiometry prototypes over a lattice-scale range before allowing any cell refinement.

The intensity diagnostics exposed why the earlier fractions drifted: the starting CIF displacement parameters and fixed polarization produced a coherent high-angle deficit, and unconstrained thermal refinement “improved” the fit only by driving Uiso outside the laboratory’s allowed 0.003–0.020 Å² range. I’m rebuilding from a clean project with phase-common Uiso fixed inside that range and reversible, bounded profile/specimen trials; the unphysical thermal solution will not be used.

A more credible cross-chemistry parent emerged from the residual ranking: K₂FeF₅ has the same 2:1:5 topology, and the substitution K→Sr, Fe→W, F→O doubles every formal charge while preserving neutrality and gives plausible Sr–O/W–O distances after lattice scaling. I’m testing its monoclinic and orthorhombic polymorphs under the same nuisance model; this is a substantially better-justified prototype challenge than accepting the pressure-only SrWO₄ decoy.

The reversible nuisance tests now replay cleanly. A modest Suortti roughness term is supported and remains interior; asymmetry is not supported, and polarization adds only a negligible improvement once roughness is present, so I’m pruning that extra degree of freedom. The weak-phase coordinate refinements also failed the replay test—the apparent gains vanished after freezing/reopening—so the submitted Sr₂WO₅ coordinates will remain the physically repaired prototype rather than an overfit powder-only distortion.

The three-phase model is now exported with fractions 75.52 wt% SrWO₄, 10.56 wt% WO₃, and 13.93 wt% provisional Sr₂WO₅. I’m at the verification step: fresh GSAS-II replay over the full original grid, CIF/project identity checks, and physical-contact/background checks. Any mismatch here will be corrected before handoff.

Completed the PXRD analysis.

Modeled crystalline fractions:

- SrWO₄ (scheelite): 75.52 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic): 10.56 wt%
- Sr₂WO₅: 13.93 wt%, provisional analogue-derived structure

Fresh full-grid GSAS-II replay gives Rwp = 15.8386% and GOF = 4.4823. The CIFs pass occupancy/contact checks, and `fit.csv` exactly matches the replayed profile.

